    <body>
      <sec id="article-131744.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Positron emission tomography&#x02013;computed tomography (PET-CT) integrates physiologic and anatomic imaging to evaluate hypermetabolic processes, playing a key role in the diagnosis, staging, and surveillance of head and neck cancers (HNC), most commonly squamous cell carcinoma associated with tobacco, alcohol, or human papillomavirus. This activity reviews PET-CT principles, including <sup>18</sup>F-fluorodeoxyglucose uptake, standard uptake value interpretation, and common sources of false-positive and false-negative findings. Participants will also gain an understanding of appropriate clinical indications guided by National Comprehensive Cancer Network recommendations, including the detection of occult primary tumors, assessment of nodal and distant metastases, treatment planning, and posttreatment surveillance. Limitations in sensitivity, specificity, and cost-effectiveness are addressed, along with a comparison with CT and MRI. This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance the learner's competence in accurate image interpretation, staging using lymph node classification systems, selecting appropriate imaging strategies to optimize diagnostic accuracy, and implementing an appropriate interprofessional approach when employing this&#x000a0;radiographic technique to guide&#x000a0;management decisions and reduce unnecessary invasive procedures.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Apply National Comprehensive Cancer Network guidelines to select appropriate indications for PET-CT in patients with head and neck cancer.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Evaluate the limitations of PET-CT to reduce diagnostic errors for head and neck cancer.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement evidence-based imaging strategies to minimize unnecessary invasive procedures for head and neck cancer.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with interprofessional team members to improve the accuracy of staging and the coordination of patient-centered care for head and neck cancer.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=131744&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=131744">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131744.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Positron emission tomography-computed tomography (PET-CT) is a radiographic technique used to diagnose, stage, and survey hypermetabolic tissue, primarily cancer. PET is used primarily to assess physiology, while CT is used primarily to assess anatomy. Combining these 2 methods can, but does not always, improve clinical outcomes (eg, survival) or the selection of&#x000a0;the least invasive treatment method.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>This paper focuses on the use of PET-CT for head and neck cancer. The term "head and neck cancer" (HNC) encompasses several types of cancer based primarily on anatomic distribution (Please refer to&#x000a0;the&#x000a0;<bold>Anatomy</bold><bold>&#x000a0;and Physiology</bold>&#x000a0;section for more information).&#x000a0;HNC has several pathophysiologic types. The majority of HNCs are due to squamous cell carcinoma (SCC), and most SCCs arise in the oropharynx. Most HNSCC is associated with tobacco and alcohol use, presenting as a mucosal primary lesion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>High-mutation-rate strains of human papillomavirus (HPV) are the next most common cause of oropharyngeal cancer. HPV-induced HNC has its own natural history: these cancers are more likely to be found in younger patients, have better outcomes than HNC not caused by HPV, and have a different staging algorithm as a result. Another category of HNC develops in lymph nodes without another discernible primary lesion, but is still SCC, as opposed to a lymphoma. Lymphomas, glandular adenocarcinomas (eg, of the thyroid and salivary glands), and skin cancers are less common types of HNC. All of these have different natural histories and treatment regimens that are not covered in this article but are reviewed in the National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) guidelines.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131744.s3" sec-type="Anatomy and Physiology">
        <title>Anatomy and Physiology</title>
        <p>Management of HNC SCC is subdivided based on the following anatomic regions of the primary tumor:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Nasopharynx</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Larynx</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hypopharynx</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Oral cavity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Oropharynx</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Nasal cavity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sinuses</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Other (eg, lymph nodes)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Because each of these different anatomic locations has its own physiologic functions and approaches to surgical revision, each location has one or more unique treatment pathways. Lymphatic spread of HNC usually follows an anatomic progression that depends on the primary HNC site.</p>
        <p>The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) and American Academy of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery (AAO-HNS) classify neck lymph nodes using 7 "levels," along with extranodal extension (ENE) as a prognostic factor for staging and treatment decisions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r4">[4]</xref> This classification is important for use in PET-CT reports to enable consistency in communication;&#x000a0;the AJCC/AAO-HNS lymph node anatomic classification is widely published and can be obtained elsewhere. The staging of HNC significantly affects the recommended treatment options and depends on which lymph nodes are involved. Therefore, the physician interpreting a PET-CT should attempt to define the primary tumor site and describe lymph node involvement or lack thereof using the AJCC/AAO-HNS lymph node anatomic classification.</p>
        <p>The FDA-approved clinical applications of PET alone or PET-CT for HNC use a clinically safe radioisotope of the element fluorine. The unstable isotope has 9 neutrons and 9 protons, giving it an atomic mass of around 18 (<sup>18</sup>F), rather than the&#x000a0;stable fluorine&#x02019;s normal atomic mass of around 19. The <sup>18</sup>F atom is chemically bonded to a glucose molecule to substitute for an oxygen atom (deoxyglucose [DG]). <sup>18</sup>FDG can be injected into patients without adverse biological effects and, as with normal glucose, localizes to hypermetabolic cells that use glucose for energy, eg, cells with cancer mutations or other causes of inflammation. <sup>18</sup>FDG undergoes normal cell membrane transport. As with glucose, cellular enzymes quickly phosphorylate <sup>18</sup>FDG after it enters the cell, which prevents its transport back out of the cell. Cells cannot use phosphorylated <sup>18</sup>FDG for glycolysis or the electron transport chain, which delays FDG metabolism for several hours and allows sufficient time for imaging without interference to image interpretation from&#x000a0;molecule breakdown or redistribution.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>Normal tissues that conduct cell membrane transport of <sup>18</sup>FDG at high rates include left ventricular myocardium, brain, gastrointestinal tract mucosa, and kidney tubules. In addition, brown fat, skeletal muscle, and breast ducts undergoing normal physiologic activity can also concentrate <sup>18</sup>FDG. Therefore, in these tissues, it can be difficult, if not impossible, to distinguish pathological from normal physiologic metabolism of <sup>18</sup>FDG.&#x000a0;If other tissue takes up <sup>18</sup>FDG, then it can be assumed that the tissue is undergoing a pathologic process, eg, benign or malignant growth, infection (eg, abscess), or another type of inflammation (eg, arterial wall atherosclerosis).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>The degree of <sup>18</sup>FDG cellular uptake can be quantified and assigned a standard uptake value (SUV). The SUV represents a relative, but not absolute, amount of <sup>18</sup>FDG uptake compared to background radiation. SUV is calculated by&#x000a0;computer&#x000a0;software based on values (body mass, <sup>18</sup>F dose, dose-to-scan time) recorded in the software at the time of the scan. SUVs vary due to scanner parameter calibrations, partial volume effects (ie, SUV becomes less accurate with smaller lesions), and patients' rate of glucose metabolism (which depends on many factors, eg, blood glucose level). Therefore, despite the name including the word &#x0201c;standardized,&#x0201d; no standard value exists to define the normal range of <sup>18</sup>FDG uptake across&#x000a0;a patient population for any given cancer or other inflammatory condition. SUVs also vary depending on the organ and physiology of the particular cancer involved. Thus, determining which SUV value should serve as the threshold between "normal" and "abnormal," or between benign and malignant tumor cell activity, remains a subjective process that leads to false-positive and false-negative scan interpretations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r7">[7]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131744.s4" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>
<bold>Initial Diagnosis</bold>
</p>
        <p>According to the NCCN, initial staging at the time of HNC diagnosis involves CT/magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). If no obvious primary site is identified on examination, a PET-CT should be ordered to identify the primary site before any further interventions, eg,&#x000a0; an examination under anesthesia (EUA), biopsy, or tonsillectomy.</p>
        <p>
<sup>18</sup>FDG PET-CT is one of the most accurate methods for diagnosing the primary HNC lesion<italic toggle="yes">. </italic>According to the NCCN, PET-CT is indicated during initial diagnosis for the following types of physical exam-occult HNC: oral cavity, oropharynx, glottic larynx, supraglottic larynx, ethmoid sinus, and maxillary sinus. Primary site disease is not visible on contrast-enhanced CT imaging and may be revealed on FDG-PET/CT.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>The main benefit of PET-CT over other modalities is superior sensitivity for identifying a lymph node as containing cancer.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Cancer Staging</bold>
</p>
        <p>The NCCN states that PET-CT should be "considered" or performed as "clinically indicated," without a conclusive recommendation. No definitive evidence has been found that PET-CT is a cost-effective measure or saves lives compared to other imaging modalities. The odds of HNC metastasis outside the head and neck, in a setting where clinical signs are absent to suggest it, are low and variable, ranging from about 5 in 100 to 20 in 100, depending on the study.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>The NCCN recommends PET-CT as an effective modality, particularly for identifying lymph node metastasis, multistation or lower-neck node involvement, or high-grade tumor histology. The NCCN cites studies suggesting that PET-CT is superior to CT/MRI for detecting locoregional nodal and distant metastases in patients with HNC. However, CT/MRI of the neck should still be considered the modality of choice for primary-site evaluation of lymph node metastasis, with PET-CT used if CT/MRI is equivocal.&#x000a0;For occult primary, the NCCN recommends performing PET-CT if level IV or V nodes are found suspicious on physical exam or other imaging. The NCCN also advises consideration of PET-CT for the evaluation of any lymph node containing HNC with an unknown primary.</p>
        <p>Given the higher incidence of lymph node spread in T3 and T4 tumors or known N1 or higher tumors, the NCCN favors PET-CT for initial workup in those cases. For locoregional advanced cancer, PET-CT is an option for evaluating distant or thoracic diseases. However, it has poor sensitivity in detecting metastases in all the previously mentioned tissues (please&#x000a0;refer to&#x000a0;the&#x000a0;<bold>Anatomy</bold><bold>&#x000a0;and Physiology</bold>&#x000a0;section for more information). For instance, in evaluating brain metastases, MRI is the more appropriate modality. Additionally, PET-CT has a low specificity for differentiating distant SCC metastases from concurrent melanomas, high-grade neuroendocrine carcinomas, and adenocarcinomas. However, even if PET-CT is positive, the NCCN guidelines 2026.1 version still recommend obtaining a diagnostic-quality CT or MRI before biopsy or resection. Notably, some hospitals perform a diagnostic-quality CT or MRI as part of the PET evaluation, while others do not.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Treatment Planning</bold>
</p>
        <p>The NCCN says, "For patients who are under consideration for a surgical primary approach, the higher sensitivity of PET-CT is warranted for tumors approaching the midline to determine the surgical approach to the contralateral neck." This also applies to radiation treatment planning.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Surveillance Using PET-CT Posttreatment/Therapy</bold>
</p>
        <p>For patients with locoregionally advanced disease who received radiation or systemic therapy as primary treatment, the NCCN recommends PET-CT "within 3 to 6 months" for assessing therapy response and the presence of residual or secondary cancer. The NCCN recommends that, "in the absence of signs of recurrence or progression," the initial posttreatment surveillance PET-CT be performed no earlier than 12 weeks to avoid false-positive interpretations, which commonly occur due to posttreatment inflammation. The guidelines say that CT or MRI can be performed in lieu of PET-CT for surveillance if there is reason to believe a clinical response has already occurred. The guidelines are based on a 2016 meta-analysis by Cheung that assessed only prospective, nonrandomized studies comparing PET-CT with other imaging options, and on a 2016 randomized trial comparing PET-CT with open surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>For HNC patients initially treated with induction chemotherapy before an attempt at definitive surgical or other treatment, the NCCN states that either PET-CT or CT may be used to monitor locoregional or distant metastasis progression to aid in the decision whether to offer an attempt at definitive therapy. After treatment with definitive therapy, chest CT and PET-CT can be used if concern for locoregional or distant disease progression is present.</p>
        <p>If, after 12 weeks, the PET-CT is ambiguous, a subsequent PET-CT or CT should be obtained if no biopsy is performed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;If, after 12 weeks, the PET-CT is negative and the patient is asymptomatic, the NCCN recommends no further imaging unless new signs or symptoms occur. PET-CT picks up residual or secondary tumors in around 10% of HNC SCC patients at 12 months and around 5% of patients at 24 months, mostly at distant sites, but for posttreatment surveillance after 6 months, no PET-CT algorithm has been proven to increase survival or other meaningful outcomes compared to CT or MRI.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;Thus, the NCCN recommends PET-CT for symptomatic patients and discourages routine surveillance imaging in asymptomatic patients after a negative 3-month posttreatment scan.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131744.s5" sec-type="Equipment">
        <title>Equipment</title>
        <p>As unstable <sup>18</sup>F decays, it releases positrons. Within about 2 mm of travel from their origination site in tissue, positrons collide with electrons and release gamma energy (photons). The photons strike scintillator crystals (ie, energy-transmitting crystals) housed in a device that encircles the patient. Scintillator crystals used in modern imaging machines vary by manufacturer, each with its own photon detection yield and resolution; a common crystal type used for PET is lutetium oxyorthosilicate. The crystals transmit the photoelectric energy into circuits that convert the electric energy into computer data.</p>
        <p>The&#x000a0;scintillation (ie, photon) detector is designed to detect only photons that simultaneously register while traveling in opposing 180-degree directions (meaning the photons must have originated from the same location in the body). Thus, a computer algorithm can be used to directly pinpoint the source of the 2 photons within several millimeters of error in 3 dimensions. The resolution of scanning using <sup>18</sup>FDG PET is typically considered to be 8 mm, meaning that a hypermetabolic tissue that is smaller than 8 mm may not be visible, or that hypermetabolic tissue shown on the scan may be as far away as 8 mm from its actual location (assuming the patient was perfectly still between the time of the CT scan and PET scan).</p>
        <p>Similarly, a CT machine envelops a patient with an external source of radiation, detects the photons that pass through the patient to a photon receptor, and produces images that demonstrate the size, shape, and composition of organs and abnormalities within the body based on the distribution of&#x000a0;absorbed&#x000a0;photons. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/130532">CT-Scan Image Production Procedures</ext-link>," for further information.</p>
        <p>At the outpatient or inpatient facility where PET-CT is performed, this modality requires an extensive list of additional equipment, including laboratory equipment for containing, measuring, and administering the pharmaceuticals involved. The liquid sample containing the <sup>18</sup>FDG injected intravenously into the patient is normally shipped to the imaging facility based on a precalculated dose per patient, because <sup>18</sup>FDG is synthesized using a particle accelerator, which very few medical institutions own. <sup>18</sup>FDG pharmacology and PET equipment are reviewed in further detail elsewhere.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;PET-CT image interpretation also depends on the type of computer software used. For example, many programs superimpose PET images onto CT images and display them in a 3D-like fashion.</p>
        <p>The American College of Radiology (ACR) published guidelines for imaging patients with PET-CT [ACR&#x02013;ACNM&#x02013;SNMMI&#x02013;SPR. <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://gravitas.acr.org/PPTS/GetDocumentView?docId=173">Practice Parameter for Performing FDG-PET/CT in Oncology</ext-link>. 2021]. The guidelines specify criteria that imaging equipment should meet or exceed, including PET scanner and CT scanner pixel resolution, photon detection sensitivity, CT slice thickness, and scan time.</p>
        <p>The ACR left open several technical parameters&#x000a0;to the discretion of the imaging facility, including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>What PET scintillation detector type and photon detection method to use</bold>: The ACR reported the absence of any head-to-head trials demonstrating one type of scanning equipment to be superior in quality and cost.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Whether to inject the same amount of <sup>18</sup>FDG for every patient or use a formula to vary the dose between patients</bold>: Dose amounts can be based on parameters that can change from patient to patient, eg, body mass, crystal photon detector settings, and percentage of scan slice overlap.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Whether to use intravenous contrast media</bold>: Contrast may or may not add diagnostic information. It can artificially lower tissue SUVs, but the impact on diagnostic interpretation is usually limited.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r15">[15]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>What injection-to-scan time to use</bold>: Most facilities obtain transmission images 60 minutes after&#x000a0;<sup>18</sup>FDG administration. However, this time may be shorter (no less than 45 minutes) or longer for clinical trials or unique clinical situations. SUVs obtained at injection-to-scan times of 60&#x000a0;minutes were found to vary little compared to injection-to-scan times of 55 to 75 minutes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r16">[16]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131744.s6" sec-type="Preparation">
        <title>Preparation</title>
        <p>The&#x000a0;2016 ACR guidelines state that the healthcare professional requesting the PET-CT should provide the imaging facility with documentation that satisfies insurance approval for the medical necessity of the exam, including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Patient signs and symptoms</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Other relevant clinical histories (eg, known medical diagnoses).</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>In addition, information regarding a provisional diagnosis and past surgeries, radiation, biopsies, chemotherapy, and other scan findings of interest should be provided. It may at times be critical to enable proper performance and interpretation of the examination.</p>
        <p>PET-CT requires patient communication and cooperation. Therefore, before arriving for the scan, the ACR recommends that the patient be instructed on the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Avoid any exercise during the 24 hours before the scan.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Avoid exposure to inflammatory substances (smoking of any kind, tobacco, alcohol) during the 24 hours before the scan.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Eat low-carbohydrate meals for at least 24 hours before the scan.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fast (including no parenteral nutrition or oral/intravenous fluids containing sugar or dextrose) for at least 4 hours before the scan.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hydrate with a goal of 1 L (34 oz) ingested within 2 hours before the scan.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r19">[19]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>For interpretation of <sup>18</sup>FDG uptake based on a normal physiologic distribution, serum glucose should be in the range of 70 to 200 mg/dL. Therefore, the ACR recommends that serum glucose analysis should be performed immediately before <sup>18</sup>FDG administration under the following conditions:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>If the serum glucose is &#x0003e;300 mg/dL, then the scan should be rescheduled.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>If the serum glucose is &#x0003e;200 mg/dL, the scan may be rescheduled, or a repeat measurement could be performed about 20 to 30 minutes later.
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>If the serum glucose level has decreased to less than 200 mg/dL, then the scan can be performed.</p></list-item><list-item><p>If the serum glucose level has decreased but remains&#x0003e;200 mg/dL, the cycle can continue until glucose is &#x0003c;200 mg/dL, or FDG can be administered at the discretion of the interpreting clinician.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>If the serum glucose is &#x0003c;70 mg/dL because the&#x000a0;patient has taken an antidiabetic medication, then waiting 30 minutes and repeating a finger-stick glucose measurement should be considered before rescheduling the exam.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>If the serum glucose is &#x0003c;70 mg/dL for another reason, the scan should be rescheduled and the underlying cause treated.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Brown fat is a subtype of fat that is relatively hypermetabolic to white fat and is stimulated by processes such as shivering or anxiety. To avoid false-positive interpretations due to brown fat uptake, the following is recommended:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Keep the patient warm (use heated blankets and a warm waiting room).</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Administer oral beta-blockers, such as propranolol 20 mg, 60 minutes before <sup>18</sup>FDG injection.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Administer intravenous narcotics, such as fentanyl, shortly before the <sup>18</sup>FDG injection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r20">[20]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>To avoid false-positive interpretations due to muscle uptake, clinicians should employ the following strategies:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Keep the patient seated or recumbent in a quiet room.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Administer oral alprazolam 0.5 mg immediately after <sup>18</sup>FDG injection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r21">[21]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>To avoid patient motion that causes the scanning equipment to produce anatomic imaging artifacts (called misregistration artifacts), the following methods are recommended:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Train the patient to use breathing and motion-reducing behaviors. Breathing should be shallow and consistent, performed the same way for both PET and CT imaging.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Train the patient to think relaxing, calming thoughts. Mindfulness techniques can help patients remain still during the 30-60 minutes of PET scanning.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131744.s7" sec-type="Technique or Treatment">
        <title>Technique or Treatment</title>
        <p>Given that the head and neck are the key areas of interest, scanning should be performed with arms down to avoid CT beam-hardening artifacts in these regions. In addition, a neck immobilization device should be used to prevent patient motion that can cause anatomic misregistration artifacts. The timing of image acquisition can be modified using breathing-gating software to correct for respiratory motion, but this technique lengthens scan time.&#x000a0;If tumor staging is the only purpose of the PET-CT, then low-dose CT can be performed. If the scan is performed for treatment planning purposes, a regular dose CT should be performed to allow diagnostic-quality anatomic resolution and interpretation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r22">[22]</xref></p>
        <p>The method used to favor a benign versus malignant process can use either an average SUV or the highest detected SUV inside the region of interest. Many clinical studies using <sup>18</sup>FDG predefined 2.5 as the critical threshold, interpreting findings as benign if the average SUV was below this value and as malignant if it was above. This strategy is based on the assumption that background tissue activity always has an average SUV below 2.5.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r22">[22]</xref></p>
        <p>However, SUV is more precisely evaluated across patients when tied to lean body mass than actual body mass. Furthermore, many radiologists believe that using an SUV threshold higher than that measured in the bloodstream is a more accurate way to predict whether a finding is malignant. Radiologists also use other factors, eg, the shape of the abnormality, to guide image interpretation. For example, linear uptake is most likely a result of a benign process, eg, postradiation inflammation.</p>
        <p>Clinicians should recognize that PET-CT interpretation of whether a finding is pathologic, physiologic, or artifactual remains a highly subjective process, notwithstanding the need to distinguish different types of pathologic processes from each other&#x000a0;(Please refer to the <bold>Complications</bold> section for more information).</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131744.s8" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Numerous situations can lead to false-positive interpretations, including image artifacts, normal tissue uptake of <sup>18</sup>FDG, and benign inflammatory processes and tumors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>Image artifacts from high-density objects (eg, metal implants or contrast) can result in falsely high SUVs (eg, due to computer attenuation correction error when the average x-ray photon energy is less than the PET&#x02019;s 511 keV photon annihilation energy). In addition, misalignment between fused PET and CT data can cause photon-attenuation correction artifacts, resulting in incorrect SUVs. For this type of artifact, the PET images can be interpreted without using computer software preset to correct for tissue photon attenuation.</p>
        <p>Normal <sup>18</sup>FDG physiologic uptake in the head and neck can occur from the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Thyroid and salivary glands</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Brown adipose and lymphoid tissue (eg, thymus)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Orbital, speech, and swallowing muscles (especially in children)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Other skeletal muscles used before or during the exam</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Smooth muscle (particularly within the gastrointestinal tract)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Muscle uptake increases in the setting of hyperinsulinemia. The tissue density&#x02019;s Hounsfield units (HU) should be measured using CT to differentiate brown fat from abnormal tissue. If the tissue's average HU is between -50 and &#x02013;150, then it can be concluded that the tissue is fat.&#x000a0;The list of inflammatory processes that can mimic cancer is extensive,&#x000a0;including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Postsurgical/biopsy inflammation, infection, or hematoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Postradiation/postchemotherapy inflammation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Inflammation from therapy is indirectly related to cancer therapy (eg, tracheostomy site, feeding, and drainage tubes)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>If patients have an active infection or a hematoma, the PET-CT scan will likely yield a false-positive result.</p>
        <p>The list of benign tumors, hyperplastic conditions (eg, bone marrow hyperplasia after chemotherapy), and dysplastic conditions (eg, fibrous dysplasia) that can increase SUVs in the head and neck tissues is also extensive.&#x000a0;The following list details situations that can lead to false-negative FDG-PET/CT interpretation:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Small lesion size (&#x0003c;2x the resolution of the imaging system, usually between 8&#x000a0;and 10 mm)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hyperglycemia and hyperinsulinemia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Recent medical therapy (chemotherapy, radiotherapy, steroid therapy)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Tumors with low metabolic activity (eg, medullary thyroid cancer and adenocarcinoma)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Other situations may occur where PET-CT can lead to a correct conclusion of malignancy, but cannot distinguish between types of malignancy. For example, lymphoma is difficult to differentiate from HNC based on imaging; the presence of associated mucosal lesions and necrotic nodes favors a diagnosis of HNC.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131744.s9" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>HNC affects many Americans yearly, primarily due to tobacco use, alcohol abuse, and HPV.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients with HNC may present with pain that is involved with the primary mass or neck mass. Patients typically have mouth or throat soreness, dysphagia, referred otalgia, and odynophagia. Most patients with HNC SCC are older than 40 years old. The following 5-year survival rates are based on the stage at which the cancer is identified:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Localized tumor only: 77% to 92%</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Regional metastasis: 38% to 60%</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Distant metastasis: 20% to 39%&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r25">[25]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>HPV-associated HNC in young patients has a better 5-year survival of 85% to 90%, so long as the patient has a lifetime tobacco history of 10 pack/years or less. If a more significant tobacco history is present, the survival rate drops dramatically.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r26">[26]</xref></p>
        <p>HNC is ideally diagnosed based on a physical exam. When this is not possible, CT with IV contrast can detect most tumors due to a disturbance in normal tissue size and density. MRI can add additional sensitivity for detecting inflammatory changes that do not result in tissue size or density changes. However, PET-CT retains the following roles in multiple circumstances for diagnosis&#x000a0;(Please refer to the <bold>Indications</bold>&#x000a0;section for more information on the adverse effects of the compound):</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>When a primary lesion cannot be discerned despite lymphadenopathy (unknown primary)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>When 1 or more lymph nodes have abnormal morphology, suspicion for cancer is low, but falsely diagnosing the lymph nodes as benign would have severe negative clinical consequences</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>When metastatic disease is suspected but not detected by other imaging modalities</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>PET-CT's primary use is in sparing unnecessary radical, modified radical, or selective neck dissections, which involve the following measures:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Radical neck dissection: excision of the ipsilateral lymph nodes in levels I to V, the sternocleidomastoid muscle, internal jugular vein, and cranial nerve XI.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Modified radical neck dissection: excision of the ipsilateral lymph nodes in levels I to V &#x000b1; cranial nerve XI.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Selective neck dissection: excision of selective ipsilateral lymph node groups.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Despite PET-CT having limited specificity for distinguishing cancer from other inflammatory processes, it can still aid in the plan for biopsying a lesion that offers the best and safest route for obtaining a tissue diagnosis.&#x000a0;When PET-CT use is limited to appropriate clinical scenarios (eg, following NCCN guidelines), the percentage of PET-CTs that add no diagnostic information compared to what could have been obtained from other less expensive alternatives can be reduced.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>For indeterminate thyroid nodules, PET-CT has been shown to reduce unnecessary surgery in up to 40% of patients and be cost-effective to that end.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;Such studies involving randomized prospective data have never been performed for HNC SCC. PET-CT&#x000a0;has never been shown to be cost-effective&#x000a0;for HNC in a prospective trial for any purpose, such as extending recurrence-free survival versus other imaging modalities or surgery alone.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;However, 3 papers using parameters for American patients have been published, all retrospective and theoretical in nature, suggesting cost-savings in 3 circumstances:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>In 2001, PET-CT was estimated to have a cost savings per quality-adjusted life year (QALY) compared with CT alone for N0 patients undergoing radiologic staging.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r31">[31]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>In 2010, PET-CT was estimated to be cost-effective for deciding for or against definitive surgery after neoadjuvant chemotherapy and radiation at a threshold QALY value of $500,000.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r32">[32]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>In 2012, PET-CT was estimated to be more cost-effective than proceeding straight to node dissection for N2 disease (spread of cancer to 1 ipsilateral 3 to 6 cm lymph node, multiple ipsilateral lymph nodes &#x0003c;6 cm, or in bilateral or contralateral lymph nodes &#x0003c;6 cm) at a cost-savings&#x000a0;per PET-CT of around $8,000.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-131744.r33">[33]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Thus, it is likely but still unproven that PET-CT is a cost-effective modality for initial staging of HNC. No literature definitively demonstrates that PET-CT is cost-effective for HNC initial diagnosis or HNC surveillance. Healthcare professionals can enhance the performance of the medical system by obtaining PET-CT only in high-pretest-probability situations, in accordance with NCCN guidelines.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131744.s10" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>PET-CT combines physiologic and anatomic imaging to evaluate hypermetabolic activity in head and neck cancer, most commonly squamous cell carcinoma associated with tobacco, alcohol, or human papillomavirus. <sup>18</sup>F-fluorodeoxyglucose accumulates in metabolically active cells, enabling detection of primary tumors, nodal involvement, and selected distant metastases. PET-CT offers high sensitivity for lymph node disease and is particularly useful in identifying occult primary tumors and guiding treatment planning. However, interpretation remains subjective due to variable standard uptake values and frequent false-positive results from inflammation or physiologic uptake, as well as false-negative findings in small or low-metabolic tumors. Appropriate use guided by&#x000a0;NCCN recommendations is essential to avoid unnecessary imaging, reduce costs, and prevent misdiagnosis or delayed care.</p>
        <p>Interprofessional collaboration strengthens diagnostic accuracy and patient outcomes through coordinated, guideline-based care. Physicians and advanced practitioners integrate clinical findings with imaging to guide staging and treatment decisions, while primary care clinicians facilitate early recognition and timely referral. Radiologists provide precise interpretation and standardized reporting, and nurses support patient preparation, education, and follow-up monitoring. Pharmacists contribute by optimizing supportive therapies and addressing treatment-related effects. Effective communication among team members promotes shared decision-making, minimizes complications, ensures appropriate surveillance, and aligns care with patient-specific risks and goals.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-131744.s11">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
